        {
          "index": 1,
          "type": "input",
          "title": "You'll want to know: 可是",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "可是 — kěshì — \"but; yet\"."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "你说得很有道理可是我不想去 nǐ shuō de hěn yǒu dàoli kěshì wǒ bù xiǎng qù — You've got a point, but I don't want to go."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "The new character, in pieces: 可 (kě) — five strokes: a long bar on top, 口 kǒu under it on the left, three strokes, and a long vertical down the right that hooks at the foot. The next lesson puts it in your hand."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 2,
          "type": "etymology",
          "title": "The word, taken apart",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "可是 (kěshì) is built from kě and 是, both inherited from Middle Chinese, khaX and dzyeX (Guangyun, in Baxter's transcription), which glosses the first 'to permit' and the second 'right, as against wrong'. How the pair came to mean but is not settled."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 3,
          "type": "notice",
          "title": "What you've built",
          "segments": [
